  "name": "Question 16",
  "problem": "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n\n$$\nr=\u27e8\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({t}^{2}-4t+8),{t}^{4}-\\frac{8}{3}{t}^{3}\u27e9,\\,t\\in [0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate the times at which the particle is stationary.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({t}^{2}-4t+8),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-\\frac{8}{3}{t}^{3})\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{2t-4}{{t}^{2}-4t+8},4{t}^{3}-8{t}^{2}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{2(t-2)}{{t}^{2}-4t+8},4{t}^{2}(t-2)\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ \u27e8\\frac{2(t-2)}{{t}^{2}-4t+8},4{t}^{2}(t-2)\u27e9 & =\u27e80,0\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(t-2)}{{t}^{2}-4t+8}=0\\, & \u21d2\\,t=2, \\\\ 4{t}^{2}(t-2)=0\\, & \u21d2\\,t=0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=2$ is the only solution that satisfies both equations, so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary.",
